# Persistence boundaries

## Repositories

- Use a repository to abstract persistence of an aggregate root when persistence is a meaningful architectural boundary.
  Do not introduce repositories merely to wrap ORM calls.

- Define repositories around aggregate roots, not database tables or every persisted entity.

- Do not introduce a generic CRUD repository as a default abstraction.
  Prefer small repositories whose operations reflect aggregate and application needs.

- Do not leak ORM query objects, persistence models, sessions, managers, or
  query-building APIs through repository contracts.
  Repository interfaces should speak in domain/application types and concepts.

- When a dedicated domain model exists, repositories should load and persist
  domain aggregates rather than expose persistence models.

- Make missing-object semantics explicit.
  Use nullable results or domain/application-specific not-found errors consistently rather than leaking persistence exceptions.

- Persist aggregate changes through aggregate-oriented operations such as `save()`;
  avoid arbitrary field-level update APIs that bypass domain behavior.

- Keep reporting, analytics, projections, and other read-optimized queries out of aggregate repositories
  when they do not load aggregates for domain behavior.
  Use dedicated query/read services for those use cases.

- Add domain-specific repository queries only for stable application needs;
  do not turn repositories into collections of one-off query permutations.

- Do not let repositories define business transaction boundaries.
  Transaction scope belongs to the application use case or unit-of-work boundary coordinating the operation.

- Introduce a Unit of Work only when explicit coordination of repositories or transaction lifecycle provides real value;
  do not add one solely to conform to an architectural pattern.

- Keep repository implementations focused on persistence and mapping.
  Business decisions and invariants belong to domain/application behavior.
